,"Method":"MTT","DOI":"10.1016/j.ejmech.2023.115233","Year":2023,"Type":"IC50","comparison":{"compound_id":"OBMD011700","measurement_id":"MCTM-040284","value":"3.99 ± 0.74","numeric":3.99}}],"logp":[{"abbreviation_in_the_article":7,"value":1.33,"value_relation":"=","std":0.08,"type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"PBS","temperature_C":37,"doi":"10.1016/j.ejmech.2023.115233","year":2023,"record_id":"MLDB-002326","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The traditional octanol-water shake-flask method was used to detected the log P values of compounds 1–8. First, n-octanol and PBS were shaken at 37 °C for 48 h to saturate each other, and then separated by separator funnel. Then, the standard curves of concentration-absorbance of compounds in two phases were established by UV spectrophotometer. The complexes 1–8 were dissolved in an equal volume mixture of mutually saturated n-octanol and PBS as described above, respectively, and then stirred in the dark for 24 h at 37 °C. The two phases were separated and determined by UV spectrophotometer, and the concentration in n-octanol (Co) and in PBS (Cw) was obtained according to the standard curves. The log P were obtained according the formula: log P = log Co/Cw."}]}